      title: "Mary Rowlandson's Captivity",
      body: `## Lancaster, February 1676

On the morning of **February 10, 1676**, a force of some four hundred Nipmuck, Narragansett, and Wampanoag warriors attacked the frontier town of **Lancaster**, Massachusetts, about thirty miles west of Boston. They besieged the fortified house of the town's minister, **Joseph Rowlandson**, who was away in Boston seeking help, and set it on fire. Inside were some thirty-seven people, including the minister's wife, **Mary Rowlandson**, about thirty-eight years old, and their three children. Twelve were killed; the rest, including Mary and her children, were taken captive.

Mary Rowlandson spent nearly **twelve weeks** as a captive, traveling with her captors through the wilderness of central Massachusetts and southern New Hampshire, as they fled from English armies. In **May 1676**, she was ransomed for twenty pounds, raised by the women of Boston, and returned to her husband. Her surviving children were also later released. In **1682**, six years after her release, she published an account of her experience: ***The Sovereignty and Goodness of God, Together with the Faithfulness of His Promises Displayed; Being a Narrative of the Captivity and Restoration of Mrs. Mary Rowlandson***. It was immediately popular, going through four editions in its first year, and is one of the most widely read works of early American literature.

## The attack

Rowlandson's narrative begins with a vivid, terrifying account of the attack:

> "On the tenth of February 1675, came the Indians with great numbers upon Lancaster: their first coming was about sunrising; hearing the noise of some guns, we looked out; several houses were burning, and the smoke ascending to heaven. … Now is the dreadful hour come, that I have often heard of (in time of war, as it was the case of others), but now mine eyes see it. Some in our house were fighting for their lives, others wallowing in their blood, the house on fire over our heads, and the bloody heathen ready to knock us on the head, if we stirred out."

(The date "1675" is in the old style, in which the year began in March; by modern reckoning, it was 1676.) Rowlandson was wounded by a bullet that passed through her side and into the arm of her youngest daughter, **Sarah**, six years old, whom she was holding. Her brother-in-law was killed, and her sister. "It is a solemn sight to see so many Christians lying in their blood, some here, and some there, like a company of sheep torn by wolves."

## The removes

Rowlandson organized her narrative not by days or weeks but by "**removes**"—the twenty times her captors broke camp and moved to a new place. The structure gives the narrative a sense of relentless, disorienting movement through an unfamiliar landscape, and of spiritual journey: each remove takes her further from her home and deeper into the "wilderness," physically and spiritually.

In the **first remove**, the captors and their prisoners camped on a hill near the town. Rowlandson describes the night as "the dolefullest night that ever my eyes saw," with the captors celebrating, "roaring, and singing, and dancing, and yelling," which made the place "a lively resemblance of hell."

## Sarah

The most heartbreaking episode of the narrative is the death of her daughter **Sarah**. For nine days, Rowlandson carried the wounded child on horseback and on foot, through the snow, without food or medicine, while the child's wound festered. On **February 18**, in the **third remove**, Sarah died in her mother's arms. "About two hours in the night, my sweet babe like a lamb departed this life." Rowlandson lay down beside the dead child all night. The next morning, her captors buried the child on a hill. "I have thought since of the wonderful goodness of God to me in preserving me in the use of my reason and senses in that distressed time, that I did not use wicked and violent means to end my own miserable life."

Her other two children were held by other captors, and she was separated from them for most of her captivity, seeing her son and her older daughter only occasionally.

## Hunger

The narrative records Rowlandson's gradual adaptation to the life of her captors. At first, she could not eat their food; but as hunger grew, she learned to eat things she had never imagined: horse's feet, bear meat, groundnuts, the bark of trees. "The first week of my being among them I hardly ate any thing; the second week I found my stomach grow very faint for want of something; and yet it was very hard to get down their filthy trash; but the third week, though I could think how formerly my stomach would turn against this or that, and I could starve and die before I could eat such things, yet they were sweet and savory to my taste." In one disturbing scene, she snatches a piece of horse's foot from a starving English child and eats it herself.

She also became a participant in the economy of the camp. She knitted stockings and sewed shirts, which she traded for food. She made a shirt for **Metacom**'s son, and Metacom himself paid her a shilling for it, and invited her to smoke a pipe with him, which she declined, having given up tobacco. He gave her a piece of bread and offered her a pancake. Her encounter with the great enemy of the English shows him as a courteous, even kindly, figure.

## Weetamoo, her mistress

Rowlandson was given to **Weetamoo**, the Pocasset saunkskwa, as a servant. Rowlandson describes Weetamoo with hostility and resentment, as "a severe and proud dame," vain about her appearance, "bestowing every day in dressing herself near as much time as any of the gentry of the land: powdering her hair, and painting her face, going with necklaces, with jewels in her ears, and bracelets upon her hands." Weetamoo struck Rowlandson and treated her harshly. From a Native perspective, as Lisa Brooks has shown, Weetamoo was a powerful political leader, and Rowlandson, a captive, was an English minister's wife who had fallen in status, whose resentment reflected her loss of her own social position. The relationship between the two women, one a captive and one a sachem, is one of the most interesting in the narrative.

> **Pause and consider.** Rowlandson describes snatching food from a starving English child. Why might she include such a painful confession in her narrative?`,
